      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14153452/assets/01902836fd1e91ac829f67a279103a9b3d5732d7b058f03c695b6733a42e673d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$",
      "worked_solution": "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14153452/assets/c058465d6ca7855446711ebc44f6b23913ee786d2fd9bb32ae6c671964e1ad32.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=1$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=1$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+x{y}^{2}+x]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{5}{4}y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{12}+(-\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
